  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>State vs central government jobs India comes down to one real trade: state civil service and
    state Group B/C posts through State PSC exams keep you inside one state, closer to family, with local
    language and administrative advantage and often more attempt chances, while central roles through
    UPSC and SSC open a wider vacancy pool, a higher pay-commission ceiling for top services, and national
    reach — at a much steeper selection rate and real relocation risk.</strong> Both run on government pay
    scales and job security, but the pay-commission timeline, the posting geography, and the promotion
    ceiling differ enough that the right choice depends on your family situation and career priorities more
    than which one "sounds" more prestigious.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Central pay follows the 7th CPC matrix on a fixed national schedule; most states adopted a broadly similar structure after 2016 but set their own fitment factor, DA, and HRA rates, and can lag the central pay-commission timeline by months or years.</li>
      <li>UPSC Civil Services recent cycles have drawn 10+ lakh applicants for roughly 800-1,000 posts, a selection rate commonly near 0.1%; state PSC competition swings from 1-3 lakh applicants in smaller states up to UPPSC/BPSC-level fields that approach UPSC's scale.</li>
      <li>Central services can still keep officers within one allotted cadre for long periods; state services remain within one state but can move you across districts. Neither guarantees your home city.</li>
