  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer to state vs central government jobs India</h2>

  <p>
    There is no universal winner here, and most comparison content treats this as "central is always
    better," which is not accurate once you look past the headline prestige gap.
  </p>

  <p>
    Central government jobs win on total vacancy pool, national reach, and pay-commission scheduling
    certainty. UPSC and SSC together recruit for a far wider range of departments than any single state can
    offer, and central pay revisions follow a fixed national timeline that every central employee gets at
    the same time.
  </p>

  <p>
    State government jobs win on geography, local advantage, and often a longer attempt runway. A State PSC
    exam usually has a narrower syllabus built around that state's own administrative structure and
    language, and many states allow more attempts before an age-based cutoff than UPSC's tighter cap.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Most "state vs central" content quietly assumes central always means IAS-level prestige and constant
      travel, and state always means staying in your hometown. Neither assumption survives contact with how
      cadre allocation and district transfers actually work. The sharper question is whether you can live
      with your specific track's real posting pattern for a career, not which label sounds bigger.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="exam-landscape">State PSC vs UPSC/SSC: the real exam map</h2>

  <p>
    Before comparing pay or postings, it helps to be precise about what each exam route actually recruits
    for, because "state government job" and "central government job" each cover more than one entry door.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>What to compare</th>
          <th>State route (State PSC)</th>
          <th>Central route (UPSC / SSC)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {examRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="What to compare"><strong>{item.aspect}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="State route (State PSC)">{item.state}</td>
            <td data-label="Central route (UPSC / SSC)">{item.central}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Inside the central route, there is a further split worth knowing before you prepare: UPSC's Civil
    Services Examination feeds All India Services (IAS, IPS, IFoS) and central Group A services, while SSC
    exams feed Group B and Group C posts across dozens of central departments — Income Tax, Customs, Central
    Excise/GST, and more. Inside the state route, most states run one state civil service exam (their own
    equivalent of the IAS/IPS combine, often called PCS) plus separate boards for state police and
    departmental posts.
  </p>

  <h3>Domicile and reservation rules you should check before you prepare</h3>

  <p>
    Most State PSC exams require domicile or long-term residency proof in that specific state — usually a
    minimum number of years of continuous residence, or a state-issued domicile certificate — before you
    can even apply. UPSC and SSC central exams carry no domicile requirement; any Indian citizen meeting
    the age and education criteria can apply from any state. Reservation categories (SC, ST, OBC, EWS,
    PwBD) apply on both tracks, but the specific percentage, the state's own OBC list, and creamy-layer
    rules can differ between a state's own reservation policy and the central government's list. Confirm
    your exact eligibility and reservation category against the specific notification, not a general
    central rule, before you commit to a state exam.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pay-scale">Pay scale: 7th CPC vs state pay commissions</h2>

  <p>
    Salary comparisons for this decision tend to quote whichever number makes one side look best. Here is
    the structural difference that actually matters, beyond any single entry-level figure.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Track</th>
          <th>Pay-scale reality</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {salaryRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Track"><strong>{item.track}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Pay-scale reality">{item.detail}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      The Eighth Central Pay Commission was constituted in late 2025 with an intended effective date of
      1 January 2026 for central employees, though actual rollout with arrears commonly runs later than
      the intended date. States are not bound to any fixed timeline for adopting the next pay commission's
      recommendations — some states have historically taken months, others several years, to match a new
      central pay structure, depending on their own fiscal capacity and political priorities.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    That timeline gap is the part most "state vs central" comparisons skip. Even when a state's pay matrix
    looks similar to the central one on paper today, the next revision cycle is not guaranteed to arrive on
    the same schedule, and DA revision dates commonly differ between the centre and individual states too.
  </p>

  <p class="note-line">
    Pay matrix levels, fitment factors, and DA/HRA rates change with each pay commission cycle and each
    state's own notifications. Treat the figures above as directional, based on the 7th CPC framework and
    recent 8th CPC formation news at the time of writing, and verify current numbers against your specific
    state's finance department notification and the latest central pay commission update before finalising
    any plan.
  </p>

  <h2 id="transfer-posting">Transfer and posting reality</h2>

  <p>
    This is the part popular assumptions about government jobs get wrong most often, in both directions.
  </p>

  <p>
    IAS, IPS, and IFoS officers are allotted to one state cadre (or a joint cadre covering a small group of
    states) early in their career, through a merit-and-preference process. Once allotted, an officer mostly
    serves inside that cadre's state for the bulk of their career, transferring between districts and
    departments within it. Central deputation — a posting to a ministry, often in Delhi, for a few years —
    is a real and valuable career step, but it happens periodically, not continuously. The image of an IAS
    officer being shuffled across the entire country every few years is broadly inaccurate for most of a
    career.
  </p>

  <p>
    Central Group A/B departmental posts recruited through UPSC's other services and through SSC (Income
    Tax, Customs, CBI, and similar) genuinely can post you to any city that department operates an office
    in, since these roles sit inside a single nationwide organisation rather than a state cadre. This is the
    part of "central government job" that most closely matches the popular image of all-India transfers.
  </p>

  <p>
    State civil service and other state-recruited posts stay within that one state's boundary for a career,
    but "within the state" still means real movement — district-level transfers every few years are a
    structural part of most state administrative roles, not an occasional exception.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">State track posting pattern</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Postings stay inside one state's geographic boundary for your entire career.</li>
        <li>District-to-district transfers happen regularly, sometimes every 2-4 years.</li>
        <li>Local language and administrative-system familiarity carry real, lasting value.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Central track posting pattern</div>
      <ul>
        <li>IAS/IPS: mostly within one allotted state cadre, with periodic central deputation.</li>
        <li>Central Group A/B departmental posts: genuinely all-India, city determined by department need.</li>
        <li>SSC-recruited Group B/C posts: assigned to a specific department's location, which can be anywhere in the country.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="prestige-ceiling">Prestige and career ceiling, honestly</h2>

  <p>
    IAS and IPS carry the strongest prestige association in Indian government service, and that reputation
    is earned by the selection rate and the policy-level responsibility that comes with the role. That
    prestige is real, and so is the difficulty of reaching it — recent UPSC cycles have selected roughly
    800-1,000 candidates out of 10+ lakh applicants, a rate commonly cited near 0.1%.
  </p>

  <p>
    What gets left out of most "central is more prestigious" framing is that this description applies mainly
    to the very top of central recruitment, not to every central government job. A central Group C post
    filled through SSC CHSL does not carry IAS-level prestige or ceiling, and comparing it against a senior
    State Civil Service officer's role on a prestige basis is not an honest comparison.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A senior State Civil Service officer with a decade of strong district-level postings usually carries
      more real administrative authority, local influence, and promotion-track credibility than a
      mid-level central Group C employee. Compare roles at similar seniority and responsibility, not the
      most senior central post against the most junior state post, if you want an honest read on ceiling.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    The genuine ceiling difference sits specifically at the top: IAS and IPS officers can eventually reach
    the most senior central secretariat, cabinet secretary-track, or director general-level roles that shape
    national policy, a level of reach a state cadre role structurally cannot match on its own. A limited
    promotion quota does let senior State Civil Service officers move into IAS after many years, narrowing
    that gap slowly for a select few, but it does not close it for most state officers.
  </p>

